{"maxId":0,"entries":{"fibromyalgia-criteria-historical-perspective|Fibromyalgia Criteria: Historical Perspective":["u65"],"patient-history|Patient history":["u92"],"nonpharmacologic-therapies|Nonpharmacologic Therapies":["u196"],"treatment-for-refractory-cases|Treatment for Refractory Cases":["u231"],"assessing-response-to-treatment|Assessing Response to Treatment":["u237"],"background-and-epidemiology|Background and Epidemiology":["u239"],"taking-the-history|Taking the History":["u271"],"diagnosing-myofascial-pain-performing-the-physical-examination|Diagnosing Myofascial Pain: Performing the Physical Examination":["u276"],"cervicogenic-headache|Cervicogenic Headache":["u301"],"deep-gluteal-pain-syndrome|Deep Gluteal Pain Syndrome":["u315"],"trapezius-mps|Trapezius MPS":["u332"],"levator-scapulae-mps|Levator Scapulae MPS":["u346"],"upper-and-lower-back-pain|Upper and Lower Back Pain":["u352"],"pseudotrochanteric-bursitis|Pseudotrochanteric bursitis":["u372"],"postherpetic-mps|Postherpetic MPS":["u382"],"addressing-precipitating-and-perpetuating-factors|Addressing Precipitating and Perpetuating Factors":["u417"],"treating-mtrps|Treating MTrPs":["u441"],"home-exercise-program|Home Exercise Program":["u447"],"other-treatments|Other Treatments":["u455"],"copy|The gold standard for diagnosing fibromyalgia (FM) is clinical evaluation by a specialist.":["u65"],"copy|Fibromyalgia patients often report that they feel pain “all over.”":["u92"],"copy|Uncommon symptoms (< 20% prevalence) include tinnitus, dizziness,":["u107"],"copy|vertigo, and Raynaud phenomenon.":["u112"],"copy|Symptoms typically begin":["u132"],"copy|within weeks to months of drug exposure and resolve with discontinuation.":["u135"],"copy|Nonpharmacologic therapies for fibromyalgia include cognitive-behavioral techniques, exercise, acupuncture, balneotherapy (ie, bathing), and massage.":["u196"],"copy|Systematic review":["u197"],"copy|of regular aerobic exercise for fibromyalgia (20 minutes daily, 2–3 times a week) demonstrated improvements in pain, function, aerobic capacity, and well-being.":["u206"],"copy|Lectures on fibromyalgia and the benefits of":["u214"],"copy|exercise improve treatment outcomes.":["u217"],"copy|Several factors limit the effectiveness of fibromyalgia treatment, including medication under-prescribing, underdosing, and/or patient noncompliance.":["u231"],"copy|Several standardized questionnaires are available to assess response to treatment.":["u237"],"copy|Myofascial pain syndrome (MPS) arises from muscle as well as surrounding fascia and is characterized by the presence of taut bands of skeletal muscle, which may contain palpable nodules (myofascial trigger points [MTrPs]; Figure 103-4).":["u239"],"copy|Surrounding alpha motor neurons in the spinal cord":["u252"],"copy|are also excited, leading to the formation of an MTrP.":["u257"],"copy|Patients with MPS may use a variety of pain descriptors— dull, aching, sharp, stabbing, burning, etc.":["u271"],"copy|Diagnosing MPS requires an accurate physical examination that relies on palpation and identification of (1) taut bands—groups of taut muscle fibers extending from MTrPs to the muscle attachments, (2) exquisitely tender, hyperirritable nodules (MTrP) in taut bands (note that taut bands may not always contain MTrPs), and (3) reproduction of the patient’s spontaneous pain with sustained pressure that may radiate distal to the MTrP (Figure 103-5).":["u276"],"copy|Cervicogenic headaches are characterized by neck and pericranial pain and are often associated with structural abnormalities of the cervical spine, including osteoarthritis and facet arthrosis.":["u301"],"copy|While cervicogenic headaches lack the pulsating quality of migraine headaches, they may have migraineassociated features such as photophobia, phonophobia,":["u303"],"copy|nausea, vomiting, and dizziness.":["u306"],"copy|When MPS occurs in the pelvic and buttock regions, the resulting pain and associated limitations in muscle function may mimic that of sciatica.":["u315"],"copy|The trapezius is a large muscle; as a result, associated MPS can have a number of presentations.":["u332"],"copy|The levator scapulae muscle extends from the upper medial border of the scapula to the transverse processes of the first four cervical vertebrae.":["u346"],"copy|These syndromes may be caused by MPS in the thoracolumbar paraspinal musculature.":["u352"],"copy|It originates from the sacrum, the iliac crest, and the spinous processes of most of the lumbar and the last two thoracic vertebrae, and inserts into the ribs, the transverse":["u355"],"copy|processes of the cervical vertebrae, thoracic vertebrae, and mastoid process.":["u358"],"copy|This syndrome is caused by MPS that involves the tensor fasciae latae (TFL) muscle.":["u372"],"copy|This is likely caused by a combination of axial spondylosis and the varicella-zoster virus (VZV) mainly in older adults.":["u382"],"copy|First, it is important to identify":["u404"],"copy|and address perpetuating factors that maintain the pain/ muscle dysfunction, and precipitating factors that initiate the syndrome.":["u407"],"copy|The factors that perpetuate MPS are typically multiple and develop over a long period of time, and each should be addressed to afford an optimal treatment.":["u417"],"copy|Efforts should be made to incorporate":["u428"],"copy|intervals of light movement or stretching if the patient is able to tolerate such activities in order to minimize overuse of small muscle fibers (ie, the Cinderella hypothesis).":["u431"],"copy|The second prong of MPS treatment is to directly treat MTrPs.":["u441"],"copy|The third prong of MPS treatment is maintaining muscular health through a home exercise program.":["u447"],"copy|A number of other treatments have been tried for MPS such as repetitive magnetic stimulation, transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS), biofeedback, massage, ultrasound, hydrocortisone/diclofenac phonophoresis, laser therapy, traditional Chinese and Japanese acupuncture, nerve blocks, and cervical spine dorsal root rhizotomy for the treatment of cervicogenic headaches.":["u455"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[11,1],[13,-2],[14,0],[40,546],[41,-1],[46,422],[47,427],[48,431],[49,434],[50,438],[51,536],[53,450],[54,456],[55,458],[56,462],[57,467],[58,471],[59,477],[60,479],[61,483],[62,487],[63,491],[64,525],[66,-21],[86,-20],[90,-19],[111,-18],[125,-17],[129,-16],[148,-15],[150,-14],[210,-12],[218,-11],[225,-10],[227,-9],[240,-8],[245,-7],[246,-6],[258,-5],[262,-4],[275,-3],[279,-2],[303,-1],[307,0],[315,1],[331,2],[344,3],[349,4],[351,5],[353,6],[366,7],[375,8],[396,9],[398,10],[407,11],[417,12],[419,13],[428,14],[433,15],[440,16],[452,116]],"preservedBreaks":{"Uncommon symptoms (< 20% prevalence) include tinnitus, dizziness, vertigo, and Raynaud phenomenon.":[65],"Symptoms typically begin within weeks to months of drug exposure and resolve with discontinuation.":[24],"Systematic review of regular aerobic exercise for fibromyalgia (20 minutes daily, 2–3 times a week) demonstrated improvements in pain, function, aerobic capacity, and well-being.":[17],"Lectures on fibromyalgia and the benefits of exercise improve treatment outcomes.":[44],"Surrounding alpha motor neurons in the spinal cord are also excited, leading to the formation of an MTrP.":[50],"While cervicogenic headaches lack the pulsating quality of migraine headaches, they may have migraineassociated features such as photophobia, phonophobia, nausea, vomiting, and dizziness.":[154],"It originates from the sacrum, the iliac crest, and the spinous processes of most of the lumbar and the last two thoracic vertebrae, and inserts into the ribs, the transverse processes of the cervical vertebrae, thoracic vertebrae, and mastoid process.":[174],"First, it is important to identify and address perpetuating factors that maintain the pain/ muscle dysfunction, and precipitating factors that initiate the syndrome.":[34],"Efforts should be made to incorporate intervals of light movement or stretching if the patient is able to tolerate such activities in order to minimize overuse of small muscle fibers (ie, the Cinderella hypothesis).":[37],"Fibromyalgia patients display comorbid pain disorders, known as chronic overlapping pain conditions, which are listed in Table 103-2.":[63],"Patients may benefit from treatment with antidepressants and some antiseizure medications, especially if they report associated symptoms such as disrupted sleep, depression, and/or anxiety (Table 103-6).":[35],"Water aerobics may benefit those who have difficulty with traditional physical therapy.":[57],"MPS can present abruptly due to acute overload, exercise (overexercise, improper body mechanics that can cause muscle sprain and strain), direct trauma, and chilling (exposure to cold temperatures especially when the muscle is fatigued).":[15],"While cervicogenic headaches lack the pulsating quality of migraine headaches, they may have migraine-associated features such as photophobia, phonophobia, nausea, vomiting, and dizziness.":[155],"They insert onto the distal third of the clavicle, the acromion, and the length of the spine of the scapula.":[33],"Patients should be encouraged to maintain an active lifestyle and incorporate a cardiovascular and aerobic fitness program into their routine.":[8]}}
